The CSS Custom Highlight API provides a mechanism for styling arbitrary text ranges on a document by using JavaScript to create the ranges, and CSS to style them.

## [Concepts and usage](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS_Custom_Highlight_API#concepts_and_usage)

Styling text ranges on a webpage can be very useful. For example, text editing web apps highlight spelling or grammar errors, and code editors highlight syntax errors.

The CSS Custom Highlight API extends the concept of other highlight pseudo-elements such as [`::selection`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/::selection), [`::spelling-error`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/::spelling-error), [`::grammar-error`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/::grammar-error), and [`::target-text`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/::target-text) by providing a way to create and style arbitrary [`Range`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Range) objects, rather than being limited to browser-defined ranges.

Using the CSS Custom Highlight API, you can programmatically create text ranges and highlight them without affecting the DOM structure in the page.

There are four steps to style text ranges on a webpage using the CSS Custom Highlight API:

1. Creating [`Range`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Range) objects.
2. Creating [`Highlight`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Highlight) objects for these ranges.
3. Registering the highlights using the [`HighlightRegistry`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HighlightRegistry).
4. Styling the highlights using the [`::highlight()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/::highlight) pseudo-element.

### [Create ranges](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS_Custom_Highlight_API#create_ranges)

The first step is to define the text ranges that you want to style by creating [`Range`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Range) objects in JavaScript. For example:

```
const parentNode = document.getElementById("foo");

const range1 = new Range();
range1.setStart(parentNode, 10);
range1.setEnd(parentNode, 20);

const range2 = new Range();
range2.setStart(parentNode, 40);
range2.setEnd(parentNode, 60);
```

### [Create highlights](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS_Custom_Highlight_API#create_highlights)

The second step is to instantiate [`Highlight`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Highlight) objects for your text ranges.

Multiple ranges can be associated to one highlight. If you want to highlight multiple pieces of text the same way, you need to create a single highlight and initialize it with the corresponding ranges.

```
const highlight = new Highlight(range1, range2);
```

But you can also create as many highlights as you need. For example, if you are building a collaborative text editor where each user gets a different text color, then you can create one highlight per user, as seen in the code snippet below:

```
const user1Highlight = new Highlight(user1Range1, user1Range2);
const user2Highlight = new Highlight(user2Range1, user2Range2, user2Range3);
```

Each highlight can be styled differently.

### [Register highlights](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS_Custom_Highlight_API#register_highlights)

Once highlights have been created, register them by using the [`HighlightRegistry`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HighlightRegistry) available as [`CSS.highlights`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS/highlights_static).

The registry is a [`Map`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map)-like object used to register highlights by names, as seen below:

```
CSS.highlights.set("user-1-highlight", user1Highlight);
CSS.highlights.set("user-2-highlight", user2Highlight);
```

In the above code snippet, the `user-1-highlight` and `user-2-highlight` strings are custom identifiers that can be used in CSS to apply styles to the registered highlights.

You can register as many highlights as you need in the registry, as well as remove highlights and clear the entire registry.

```
// Remove a single highlight from the registry.
CSS.highlights.delete("user-1-highlight");

// Clear the registry.
CSS.highlights.clear();
```

### [Style highlights](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS_Custom_Highlight_API#style_highlights)

The final step is to style the registered highlights. This is done by using the [`::highlight()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Selectors/::highlight) pseudo-element. For example, to style the `user-1-highlight` highlight registered in the previous step:

```
::highlight(user-1-highlight) {
  background-color: yellow;
  color: black;
}
```

### [Accessibility](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS_Custom_Highlight_API#accessibility)

The custom highlights do not inherently provide semantic meaning in the document structure. The [`type`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Highlight/type) attribute exposes semantics (such as spelling or grammar errors) to assistive technologies, but support may vary across platforms and the type used.

If compatibility with assistive technologies is a concern, consider using semantic HTML elements such as [`<mark>`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/mark) or providing additional accessible cues for highlights that communicate important information.

## [Interfaces](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS_Custom_Highlight_API#interfaces)

[`Highlight`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Highlight)

This interface is used to represent a collection of ranges to be styled on a document.

[`HighlightRegistry`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HighlightRegistry)

Accessible via [`CSS.highlights`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CSS/highlights_static), this [`Map`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map)-like object is used to register highlights with custom identifiers.

